WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.135
-Oh, my gosh.
-Hello.

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.803
-Thank you so much
for being here

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:05.271
and talking to me tonight.

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:07.807
I really -- I want
to get into so many things.

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:08.874
I think you're...
-Yeah.

00:00:08.874 --> 00:00:10.677
-...doing so much
good work right now.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:12.645
But...
-[ Breathes deeply ]

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:13.713
-Now, we're taping this

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.782
before the final
presidential debate airs.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:18.184
-Yeah.
-But if you could look

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.887
into the future,
what do you think your reaction

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:24.924
will be to tonight's debate?

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:28.761
-I think I'm gonna be
in the other room,

00:00:28.761 --> 00:00:33.032
screaming into the TV room,
"What's happening?"

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:35.034
Because I am getting --

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.136
I'm so anxious
about all of this.

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:41.173
It happened
in the last debate, too.

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:45.411
That fool started screaming
and talking over everybody,

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:46.579
and I had to walk out.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.214
It just made me
too upset and anxious.

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.716
-That was
one of the worst things --

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.784
I just kept going,
"How we doing? How we doing?"

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:52.952
-Yeah.
-And then I just stayed out.

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.453
And that's what I'm gonna do
tonight, too.

00:00:54.453 --> 00:00:56.389
-'Cause, obviously,
I have to watch him

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:57.823
because we have to make jokes
about this,

00:00:57.823 --> 00:00:59.492
you know, afterwards.
-Right.

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:00.760
-And it's like, just --

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.062
I still have a crick in my neck
from that.

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.999
'Cause it was like watching
two people get into an argument

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:09.301
for an hour or two --
or two hours?

00:01:09.301 --> 00:01:11.137
I go, "This is the worst thing
I ever had to watch."

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.304
I go -- just --
-It was bad.

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:13.973
-It stressed me out.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:16.009
I'm so happy
this is the last one tonight.

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:17.643
-Yeah, me, too.
-But you've gotten to know

00:01:17.643 --> 00:01:20.814
Vice President Biden personally
over the past few years.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:23.749
-Yeah.
-When did you first meet him?

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:26.886
-I first met him --

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:29.655
I first really met him
when I was doing "Veep."

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:31.824
And we were in D.C.,

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:36.695
and he invited me
to the White House for lunch.

00:01:36.695 --> 00:01:38.965
-No.
-And -- yeah.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:41.567
And I have to tell you,
it was so --

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:45.504
I was so excited and so --
I had such butterflies.

00:01:45.504 --> 00:01:48.308
And I got there -- and it was
in his office, by the way.

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:50.075
It was in his office.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:01:50.075 --> 00:01:52.278
-And so -- I know.
And then the waiter came in.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:53.612
-Yeah.
-And he said,

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:55.848
"What would you like?"
and he presented with a menu.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:57.750
And I --
I mean, I couldn't eat

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.186
'cause I was so overcome
that I was even there.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:01.520
Right?
-Of course.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.691
-And so I said, "Okay.
I'll just have that sandwich."

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.092
You know,
I'm not really thinking.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:09.095
And then Joe goes, "Oh, no.

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:10.396
You should get
the crab cakes, too.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:11.764
Bring her the crab cakes, too."

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:12.899
And then, the next thing I know,

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:15.434
I've got these two
huge plates of food,

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:18.804
and it's just me
and Joe Biden sitting there.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:23.276
And I need to make a dent
in both of these meals.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:26.312
And I had no --
I was like dry mouth, you know?

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.847
That kind of nervousness.
-How cool is that?

00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:30.750
-But he couldn't
have been nicer.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.685
Yeah, it was crazy.
And then he had to go

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:35.187
to some meeting after this,
and he says,

00:02:35.187 --> 00:02:36.889
"I got to go to a meeting.
I'm gonna go in the motorcade.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:38.191
Do you want to ride
in the motorcade?"

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:39.792
I was like, "Yeah, I want
to ride in the motorcade."

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:42.428
-You're kidding me!
-No!

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:43.796
-You were in the car with him?
-I think I took a --

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:46.065
I took a picture of it.
I think you guys may have it.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.533
But anyway, yeah.
-Oh.

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:49.468
-Yes!
That's the motorcade.

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:50.570
-How cool --
-That's the motorcade.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.607
Isn't that neat?
-At the DNC --

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.209
You got to speak at the DNC,
which is --

00:02:57.209 --> 00:02:59.779
Talk about pressure right there.
-No kidding.

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:03.382
-You told the story about the
time that Vice President Biden

00:03:03.382 --> 00:03:06.352
called you
after your cancer diagnosis.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:10.089
-Yes. I got -- I was diagnosed
with breast cancer,

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:13.893
and this is, I guess --

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:16.128
Geez, I guess it's a little over
three years ago now.

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:18.063
And he called.

00:03:18.063 --> 00:03:20.165
And, of course,

00:03:20.165 --> 00:03:24.237
his beloved son Beau...
-Yeah.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:26.305
-...had struggled
with this disease.

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:31.411
And so he was very
well connected to doctors.

00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:34.580
He knew this hospital
and that group

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.116
and this research team,
and blah, blah, blah.

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:40.320
And so he was calling me
to talk to me about

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.155
who my doctors were
and how could he help

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:45.525
and should he put me in touch
with this, that, and the other.

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:49.862
And it was just so
beyond the beyond,

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.897
in terms of kindness, for real.

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:57.370
It was just --
It was astoundingly thoughtful.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.671
-Wow.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:00.339
-And I couldn't get him
off the phone, by the way.

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:01.674
-Yeah.
-I mean, I was like,

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.475
"You've got to -- Don't you have
to run the country?"

00:04:03.475 --> 00:04:05.044
-"You have things to do, buddy."
Yeah.

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:07.046
-Yeah, totally.
-He's that amazing.

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:09.682
-But I mean, he really wanted
to talk to me about sort of

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.652
my medical plan
and how I was gonna do it

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:14.587
and blah, blah, blah.
It was incredibly sweet.

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:16.589
Needless to say,
that's certainly not the reason

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.124
he has my support.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:22.861
But it is emblematic of the
thoughtfulness and the kindness

00:04:22.861 --> 00:04:24.797
that is a part
of his personality,

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:29.035
which is sorely missing
right now in the White House.

00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:31.870
-You know, there have been
countless comparisons

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:36.075
between the current presidency
and your show, "Veep."

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.710
But I think I agree with you.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.945
I think I know
what you're gonna say.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.714
But it's almost like
what's happening

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:43.916
couldn't even
be written in "Veep."

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.151
'Cause it's too ridiculous.

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:47.086
-Well, yeah.
I mean, I got to tell you,

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:48.520
it's one of the reasons
we ended the show.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.356
Because, uh...
-Is that right?

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:54.494
-The -- Yeah.
Because the Trump administration

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:58.030
was sort of out-"Veep"-ing us
at every moment.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.499
They were sort -- They were
doing a better version

00:05:00.499 --> 00:05:04.003
of our show, except that it
wasn't even remotely funny.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.772
-[ Laughs ]
-But they really were.

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:10.409
And I mean, I can tell you
that if my writers

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:13.746
had put into a script
some of the shenanigans

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:16.815
that this guy is up to
these days,

00:05:16.815 --> 00:05:19.485
I would've said,
"Guys, not plausible.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.987
Nobody's gonna believe it.
It's too broad."

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:23.356
-Yeah.
-"It's too cartoony."

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:24.790
-Exactly.
-You know.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.758
-So many things, you go,
"There's no way this is real."

00:05:26.758 --> 00:05:28.194
But, yeah.
-I know.

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.496
-If you wrote it in a script,
you go, "That's too farfetched."

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:33.833
-Oh, too farfetched, for sure.
-You had a great tweet after --

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.201
There was a video
of Trump dancing

00:05:35.201 --> 00:05:37.236
at a rally that went viral.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.805
And he was doing this thing.
And you said,

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:41.006
"First of all, he stole 'Veep,'

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:42.341
now he's stealing
from 'Seinfeld.'"

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:43.976
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:44.977
-So good.
By the way --

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:46.245
-Melania deserves an Emmy

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:48.247
for acting like
she's attracted to this.

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:50.549
-How did you come up
with that dance, by the way?

00:05:50.549 --> 00:05:52.718
I know everyone probably asked
you about this, but how?

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.286
Is it -- it's the funniest thing
I've ever seen.

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:57.889
-Well, this is how it happened.
This is how it happened.

00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:00.493
So, the writers
wrote the script.

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:02.394
I got it the night before
the table read, right?

00:06:02.394 --> 00:06:04.764
And it said, you know,
"Elaine dances weird,"

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:06.531
but they didn't say how
or anything.

00:06:06.531 --> 00:06:08.634
So I thought, "Uh..."

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:10.169
So I got
in front of the mirror,

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:12.204
and I tried out
a couple of movements

00:06:12.204 --> 00:06:15.441
that I thought looked horrible.
-Yeah.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:17.342
-And then,
I came down to the kitchen.

00:06:17.342 --> 00:06:18.745
And my husband was there.

00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:20.546
And my mom was visiting
at the time.

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:23.983
And I said, "Okay, guys, tell me
which one of these is worse."

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:25.618
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:28.788
-And I did it for them,
and they chose that one,

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:30.356
and that's the one
that I did in the show.

00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:33.559
And the thing that most people
don't know is that, of course,

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.996
there's music playing when
you watch the -- that episode.

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:39.999
Elaine is dancing to music.
But when we actually shot it,

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:43.602
we couldn't play music
because, all of a sudden,

00:06:43.602 --> 00:06:47.406
those hurky-jerky movements
would have been to the beat.

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:48.774
I couldn't separate it.

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:49.942
You know what I mean?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.578
You would be too on rhythm.
Yeah -- oh, gosh.

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:54.413
-And it had to be
completely off-rhythm.

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:56.716
And that's just a way of me
defending myself

00:06:56.716 --> 00:06:58.651
so you don't think
that I really dance like that.

00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:01.387
I actually do have
a little bit of rhythm.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.088
-Oh, yeah.
-Not tons, but I've got it.

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:04.323
-Well, that must be --

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:05.991
Do people ask you to
do the dance all the time?

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:07.160
Like, "Hey, do the dance.
Come on."

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.028
-Yes, they do.
Yeah.

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:10.930
And I don't do it.
I don't do it.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.231
-Of course not.
-But I can tell you if I go to,

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:16.435
like, a wedding or something
where there's dancing,

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:19.271
and I hit the dance floor,
I feel all eyes on me.

00:07:19.271 --> 00:07:20.706
It's a very
self-conscious thing.

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:22.741
And so there's massive pressure
to really move...

00:07:22.741 --> 00:07:25.478
-"That was a bit I did, guys." -
-...like I know what I'm doing.

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:26.612
-And then you go,
"This is not fun."

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:29.115
Everyone's staring.
Oh, my gosh.

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:31.784
Speaking of "Seinfeld,"
you're planning a mini reunion

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.786
for a fundraiser
happening tomorrow night.

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:35.054
-I want to talk about that
when we come back.

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:36.556
Is that cool?
-Great.

